A||||16||turbo86se||HELP! Fiero broke down!||||1||
Z||000000||turbo86se||10-25-2001||01:52 PM||||My trusty '88 coupe broke down this afternoon! I was driving home about 45mph, T-Tops off, enjoying the 87 degree weather, when the Fiero made a bump and snap, it felt like I ran over a piece of tire, or had a flat tire. I looked in my rearview, nothing. So I didn't run over anything. So the front right wheel locked up, I pulled over immediately to a screeching halt along busy 17 Buisness. The front right wheel locked up. It didn't look slanted, I looked underneath and couldn't see anything out of the ordinary. <BR>If I had been on the highway doing 70-75 like I normally do, this could have been ugly. I don't know why I took buisness, no special reason.<P>Could anyone help? I'm thinking it's the balljoints. BTW, this car has been wrecked, on the front right side. Inner tie rod and upper control arm have been replaced. Car has 125K miles, suspension is original as far as I know. <P>I'm waiting for the tow truck to come and pick it up and get it to a local repair shop.<P>Please help!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tigger||10-25-2001||01:55 PM||||Front wheel bearing went ka-put, other than that the caliper locked (highly doubt).  Need to check that the spindle didn't get chewed up, may have to replace both.||||reg||1
Z||000002||turbo86se||10-25-2001||02:00 PM||||Thanks tigger for the quick reply.<BR>Tell me more about the front wheel bearing.<BR>Is it hard/expensive to replace?<BR>Is this something that'll keep my Fiero off the road for a while?<BR>Thanks again!||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||10-25-2001||02:01 PM||||Joel,<P>If the balljoint snapped you would have definitely noticed your coupe listing to one side!  It's still possible that teh lower joint snapped, but you shoule see the top of the wheel/tire pointing inward.<P>Another possibility is a caliper locked up on you or the caliper fell off.  Have you recently worked on the brakes?  It's possible that one of the bolts that holds the caliper to the spindle backed out and when you went to apply the brakes the caliper flew off or pivoted on the one remaining bolt until it wedged itself between the spindle and wheel.<P>This happened to me about 7 or 8 years ago after I did a brake job.  I had just existed the highway from a 120MPH blast and hit the brakes to slow down and Whammo!  The right front caliper came loose, broke in half and wedged between the spindle and wheel.  It shattered teh rotor, bent the upper control arm & spindle and took a layer of aluminum off of the wheel.  <P>Hopefully it is nothing serious....obviously you won't know much until you remove the wheel and take a look.  <P>Let us know what you find out.<P><BR>Mike<P><BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfiero||10-25-2001||02:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by turbo86se:<BR><B>Thanks tigger for the quick reply.<BR>Tell me more about the front wheel bearing.<BR>Is it hard/expensive to replace?<BR>Is this something that'll keep my Fiero off the road for a while?<BR>Thanks again!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>'88 front wheel bearings are sealed (like the rear) and rarely fail, at least to the extent of locking up.  I have only replaced one '88 wheel bearing in 11 years and it was just a little sloppy.<P>Still, if you do need a wheel bearing they are pretty easy to change out.  I highly recommend using an impact wrench since those bolts are on there tight.  It should only take 1-2 hours tops to replace one, so it isn't that difficult and is within the scope of a 'do-it-yourselfer'.<P>New ones are impossible to locate and expensive if you do find one.<P>I have some good used ones if you need one.....LMK.  <P>    <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jefrysuko||10-25-2001||02:07 PM||||Have you looked behind the wheel yet?  You could have simply had some road debris get trown into the wheel and locked it up similary to how mrfiero said.  I had this happen on another car from a piece of truck tire lying on the road.  I simply had to take the tire of my car and remove the debris.  Nothing was damaged on the car.||||reg||1
Z||000006||turbo86se||10-25-2001||02:08 PM||||Hi Mike,<BR>When the car came to a rest, I was on the downslope of a ditch. So I really couldn't tell. If anything, the wheel was pointed inwards. The brakes haven't been worked on in about two years. Looks like I'll be needing some parts from you!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tigger||10-25-2001||02:13 PM||||That was what I was thinking mrfiero, if the balljoint broke the wheel would be at an angle.  Hope a caliper never goes out on me like that.<P>I wasn't thinking, '88s had sealed bearing hub units so you won't have to worry about the spindle.<P>Good chance the bearing had been damaged in the accident you mentioned, wasn't replaced, and finally gave up.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||turbo86se||10-25-2001||02:21 PM||||The shop manager just called me, my Fiero is on the lift and secure. It turns out I've got a broken right front caliper. Also, the two bolts that hold the caliper to the steering knuckle are gone. <P>I've been quoted a price of $70 for the reman caliper and $24 for the bolts. I know Fiero store has the bolts for $4.95 and caliper for $80. <P>Can anyone get me a good F/R '88 caliper?<BR>Thanks again everyone!!||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfiero||10-25-2001||02:22 PM||||OK....LMK what you need, I have it!<P>I have snapped 2 balljoints in the past 11 years.  One was on my first Fiero (an '88 GT)......I was backing out of a parking spot when *snap*.  The lower joint snapped at the base and the wheel was pointing inward.  I was less than 2 blocks away from a repair shop, but it was not even remotely driveable, so a $75 tow was needed.<P>I also snapped one while driving about 50 MPH in an '87 coupe.  I was going around a bend and the right wheels went off the road just a bit......of all places to go off it was in front of a HUGE pothole.  The balljoint busted in half and I had to fight to keep it on the road. <P>As afr as my brake mishap.......I had problems with the pads and had removed the caliper 5 or 6 times to get everything just right.  By that time I was too lazy or tired to double check things and as a result the lower bolt backed out and the rest is history.  It cost me about $400 to fix....all because I didn't check the torque.  <P>Joel.....LMK what you need as soon as you know.   ||||reg||1
Z||000010||DRH||10-25-2001||02:23 PM||||My guess is a brake caliper problem. I can't see a bearing just locking up without making a LOT of noise for quite a while first. <P>BTW, you can still get new front wheel bearing assemblies from <A HREF="http://www.autozone.com" TARGET=_blank>www.autozone.com</A>  for $93. I bought 2 last spring. I also checked for someone else and they still had them a month or so ago.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||DRH||10-25-2001||02:26 PM||||Wow the responses are coming so fast this is starting to look like live chat!<P>So it was the caliper.||||reg||10
Z||000012||mrfiero||10-25-2001||02:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by turbo86se:<BR><B>The shop manager just called me, my Fiero is on the lift and secure. It turns out I've got a broken right front caliper. Also, the two bolts that hold the caliper to the steering knuckle are gone. <P>I've been quoted a price of $70 for the reman caliper and $24 for the bolts. I know Fiero store has the bolts for $4.95 and caliper for $80. <P>Can anyone get me a good F/R '88 caliper?<BR>Thanks again everyone!!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I had already sent my other reply before I saw this......$70 for a remanufactured caliper is reasonable.  I have had good luck with Raybestos calipers (I have these on my GT).<P>I also have some bolts.....I'll just give them to you if you need them.<P>Make sure you or the shop thoroughly inspect the spindle.......it is cast iron, but can bend/deflect under high loads.  When my caliper fell off I bent it enough to where it could not be properly aligned.  Plus you need to determine just why the bolts came out......if the spindle is the culprit then you will need to replace it.<P><BR>I just sold a R/F '88 caliper, but I just might have another one kicking around.  Let me check and I will get back with you(probably in an hour or 2).<P>   <P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||turbo86se||10-25-2001||02:30 PM||||yeah you're right DRH!<BR>I love this Forum!<P>Mike, thanks for looking. LMK as soon as you find out. Call me if you need to at 843-446-4431<P>TIA!<BR>-joel<p>[This message has been edited by turbo86se (edited 10-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Tigger||10-25-2001||02:37 PM||||It was the caliper after all, hoped it wasn't and just an exploded bearing.<P>You may want to check into a new spindle now.  If the threads for the caliper bolts were bad it could have been the reason they backed out.<P>Yea, the forum is great!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000015||mrfiero||10-25-2001||02:57 PM||||Joel,<P>I just got back from my storage unit and I have good news and bad news.<P>First the good news.....I have a R/F caliper!  <P>Bad news......I may have it sold already (buyer wants a pair of front calipers).  <P><BR>As soon as I hear back from him regarding whether or not he still wants/needs the calipers I will get back to you.<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Raydar||10-25-2001||03:30 PM||||I've heard of these bolts backing out, on several other 88s. Need to use Loctite when they are reassembled. Takes a Torx T-55. <P>EDIT - for correct Torx size. Thanks, Mike V. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-26-2001).]||||reg||1
